course: dictionary
lesson: 10329
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word trail can mean a path, a line of marks, or to follow behind. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «Stay on the trail, and tell an adult your route.» Here a trail is a path through the countryside, because you walk along it and tell someone where you are going. Check by swapping in your meaning: a path fits, so that is the right one.
explain: «Meteor showers happen when the Earth passes through trails of dust left by comets.» Here a trail is a line of marks or signs left behind. Ask yourself: what would fit here? A line of dust left behind fits, so that is the meaning.
fact: Trail can be a noun (a trail, trails) or a verb (trail, trails, trailed, trailing).
fact: As a noun, a trail is a path through the countryside, a forest or mountains.
fact: As a noun, a trail can also be a line of marks or signs left by someone or something, like footprints.
fact: As a verb, to trail is to follow behind someone, or to drag along the ground behind.
fact: In a game, to trail is to be losing.
exercise: «Stay on the trail, and tell an adult your route.» In this sentence, trail means | A line of footprints left behind | To drag something along the ground | *A path through the countryside | To be losing
exercise: «The snail's slime trail helped it find its way home.» What is a trail here? | A tent in the forest | *A line of marks left behind | A team that is losing | A kind of hiking boot
exercise: «The team was trailing by two points.» What does trailing mean? | Walking in front of the others | Shouting loudly | Drawing a picture | *Being behind in the game
test: «A detective followed a trail of clues.» What does trail mean here? | *A line of signs left behind by someone | Lost in a game | A path through a forest | To drag a rope along the ground
test: Which meaning of trail is to follow behind someone? | A path up a mountain | *To walk behind someone | Footprints in the snow | Losing a race
exam: «A boy in Montana sees a bear at the edge of the trail.» What does trail mean here? | Being behind in a game | *A path through the countryside | A line of dust left by a comet | To drag something along the ground
